{"chunk_id": "20fa43f6aab8", "text": "King Yayati, cursed with premature old age, asks his sons to exchange their youth for his decrepitude. Drahyu and Anu refuse, fearing the humiliations and limitations of old age, and Yayati curses them in anger. His youngest son, Puru, accepts the burden with humility, giving his youth to his father. Restored to vigor, Yayati spends a thousand years enjoying worldly pleasures while still ruling righteously and performing religious duties. When the thousand years end, he realizes a profound truth: desire is never satisfied by indulgence; it only grows stronger. Recognizing the endless hunger of worldly craving, Yayati renounces pleasure, returns youth to Puru, and prepares for a life of spiritual withdrawal. The episode illustrates a central moral theme of the epic\u2014the futility of unchecked desire and the value of duty, sacrifice, and renunciation.\nYayati curses sons who refuse his old age, Puru accepts it, Yayati enjoys youth for a millennium, then renounces desire and restores Puru\u2019s youth and kingdom.\nThemes: filial duty, curse and blessing, insatiable desire, renunciation, kingship succession", "vector": [0.01805415004491806, -0.04038679227232933, -0.0006855467217974365, 0.002077930374071002, -0.0200162623077631, 0.031012261286377907, -0.023926857858896255, -0.002289129886776209, -0.015192737802863121, -0.016759701073169708, 0.015179111622273922, 0.01404817309230566, 0.026202362030744553, 0.007242098916321993, 0.005889740772545338, -0.0006157146417535841, 0.041558608412742615, -0.023763349279761314, 0.03910597041249275, -0.015206363052129745, -0.013782470487058163, -0.0037913715932518244, -0.022632410749793053, -0.00348308845423162, -0.023477207869291306, 0.014293436892330647, 0.011139069683849812, -0.030194714665412903, 0.028423363342881203, -0.010430529713630676, -0.011949803680181503, -1.0452192327647936e-05, 6.983208731980994e-05, 0.01779526099562645, -0.017522744834423065, -0.0007123724790289998, -0.006111159